## The Year of Living (Un)safely
2026 has already seen a total of 337 food recalls, with 91 of them classified as Class I--the riskiest category, posing serious health risks or even death. That's nearly one Class I recall every two days.

## Paints in Your Pudding?
On May 6th, Guangdong Zonegoing Food Co., LTD faced several Class II recalls for its 52USA brand Popping Boba products. The reason? Unapproved colors like tartrazine (FD&C Yellow 5), sunset yellow (FD&C Yellow 6), and allura red (FD&C Red 40). Consumers should not have to worry about ingesting industrial-grade coloring agents when looking for a sweet treat.

## A Crack in the Crème Brûlée
Also on May 6th, LACTALIS CANADA, INC pulled its Specially SELECTED brand Vanilla Creme Brulee off Aldi shelves for potentially containing glass. Dessert with a crunch has never been so dangerous.

## Brand to Watch Out For
Moonlight Packing Corporation tops this month's "list of shame" with 11 Class I recalls in 2026 alone. Their record of 96 (an F-grade) makes it clear they have some quality checks to implement.

## Tips to Play it Safe
  - Always check the FDA website for updated recalls.
  - If in doubt, throw it out. Your health is not worth the risk.

## Concluding Thoughts
The data is clear: food recalls are not a rarity but an ongoing reality. The best defense is vigilance and making informed decisions about what you eat and where you source it.
